{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RCA (rebar-debug Phase 1 complete — proven, cited root cause)\n\nPROBLEM: tests/interfaces/io/test_export_ndjson_js_safe.py::test_export_timestamps_round_trip_exactly flakes ~1/256.\n\nPROVEN ROOT CAUSE (test over-assertion / data-dependency — NOT a serialization/product bug):\nThe test's load-bearing premise `assert int(float(expected)) != expected` (line 126) REQUIRES the stored ns timestamp to be float64-INEXACT so it can demonstrate the naive-float64 hazard. The `ns_ticket` fixture stamps created_at/updated_at from the real wall-clock via `time.time_ns()` (~1.79e18). In [2^60, 2^61) the float64 grid spacing (ULP) is 256, so an integer is float64-exact iff it is ≡ 0 mod 256 — i.e. ~1/256 of wall-clock ns draws. When the draw is exact, `int(float(expected)) == expected`, the premise is False, and the test fails with \"stored updated_at/created_at <n> survives a float64 round-trip; the hazard is undemonstrable\".\n\nCONTRACT CITATION (rules out a product bug): src/rebar/schemas/ticket_state.schema.json defines created_at/updated_at as nanoseconds-since-epoch INTEGERS whose wire form is the EXACT decimal STRING (INVARIANT: \"the conversion is lossless — the decimal digits are identical to the integer form; only the JSON type differs\"). The export path emits those exact digits; there is NO float64 anywhere in the store or export path. The float64 appears ONLY inside the test, modeling what a naive consumer would do. So the serialization is correct and the store is canonical int — the defect is the test's data-dependent premise, not precision loss. Close class: `flaky`.\n\nPROOF (deterministic runtime reproduction): the physical clock has a documented test seam `REBAR_HLC_NOW` (src/rebar/_store/hlc.py physical_now; next_tick = max(state, witness, physical_now())+1). Injecting `REBAR_HLC_NOW=1788072768731609343` forces the CREATE tick to 1788072768731609344 (a multiple of 256 → float64-EXACT), and the target test fails DETERMINISTICALLY with the exact CI message and value:\n  \"AssertionError: stored created_at 1788072768731609344 survives a float64 round-trip; the hazard is undemonstrable\" — matching Gerrit 2419 patchset-2 CI run 33297528057. Measured flake fraction over 200k near-range draws: 0.399% (~1/256), confirming the rate.\n\nATTRIBUTION (caused_by = a8db-dc3c-983a-40b0, \"guilty-pusslike-wyvern\"): git blame shows the entire test file — both the data-dependent premise assertion and the wall-clock `ns_ticket` fixture — was introduced whole by commit 36ae045f41c38c82fe58b9cd85af6882e9a890c3 (\"fix(io): JS-safe ns timestamps in export NDJSON\", rebar-ticket: a8db-dc3c-983a-40b0). That is the fix site AND the cause site (the fragile assertion shipped with that ticket's \"new regression tests\"), so this is a same-commit origin, not an unmet-obligation case. A reviewer of a8db could plausibly have caught the wall-clock data dependency. Will be recorded via --caused-by on close.\n\nFIX (test-file-only; outside eb64's domain of _store/ / reconciler / src/rebar/llm/): make the hazard deterministic per the ticket's suggested scope — the `ns_ticket` fixture injects `REBAR_HLC_NOW` = a fixed multiple-of-256 base so stored created_at (base+1) and updated_at (base+2) are DETERMINISTICALLY float64-inexact, plus a fixture precondition asserting inexactness and a dedicated regression-guard test. The lossless-string assertion is NOT weakened."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788088638393209891,"uuid":"80d566e4-9653-4d65-86db-d98b7fbd2931"}